## Mandatory development workflow: icca-harness

For every task that may modify production code or create a commit, you
MUST load and follow the `icca-harness` skill BEFORE making
the first production-code change.

Applies to: features, bug fixes, refactorings, behavior changes, API
changes, migrations, production configuration changes, and commits
containing any of the above.

The workflow may not be silently abbreviated or replaced by an ad-hoc
implementation/review cycle. For non-exempt tasks: the orchestrator
owns specification, acceptance tests, verification, staging and
commits; implementation is delegated to a subagent with model:
"opus"; the implementer never modifies frozen acceptance tests,
never stages, never commits; no commit without a passed commit gate.

Only exempt: research/analysis with no production-code changes,
documentation-only work, and trivial typo/comment-only changes.
Before using an exemption, explicitly classify the task as exempt,
state why, and run:
`python3 ~/.claude/red-proof/red_proof.py exempt --reason '<why>'`

Enforcement: global PreToolUse hooks block production-code edits
without an active cycle and `git commit` without a passed commit
gate. State and evidence live in `~/.claude/red-proof/`.
